s, they have a chi on T5-T7, so maybe not. \n\nNow, considering my hand structure. I have three chows already. To win, I need another meld and a pair. Wait, my hand has five tiles left. Wait, the initial hand is 13 tiles, and each meld takes three tiles. So with three chows (each three tiles), that's 9 tiles. So my hand has 13 - 9 = 4 tiles? Wait, the user says my hand tiles are [W1:1; W2:1; T1:1; T3:1; T6:1]. That's five tiles. Wait, that doesn't add up. Wait, maybe I have three melds (each three tiles) and five tiles in hand. So total is 3*3 +5=14, which is correct for a winning hand. So the five tiles are what's left. So I need to form another meld and a pair. \n\nLooking at my five tiles: W1, W2, T1, T3, T6. Let's see possible combinations. \n\nIf I can form a chi or pung with these. \n\n- W1 and W2: Could there be a chi? If I have W1 and W2, I need W3 to make a chi. But I don't have W3. So no. \n\n- T1 and T3: If I have T1 and T3, maybe with T2 to make a chi. But I don't have T2. \n\n- T6 is a single tile. \n\nSo maybe I need to form a pair. Let's see. \n\nLooking at the five tiles, are there any pairs? No, all are singles. So I need to form a pair and another meld. \n\nWait, perhaps I can form a pung or kong? But I only have one of each tile. \n\nSo, the only way is to form another chi and a pair. \n\nLooking at the five tiles, possible chis:\n\n- T1, T3: If I can get T2, but I don't have it. \n\n- W1, W2: Need W3. \n\n- T3 and T6: Not consecutive. \n\nSo maybe there's no way to form a chi with the current tiles. Therefore, I need to discard a tile to leave four tiles, which can form a pair and a chi. \n\nWait, if I discard one tile, I have four left. Let's see:\n\nIf I discard T6, remaining tiles: W1, W2, T1, T3. Can I make a pair and a chi? \n\nPossible pairs: none. All are singles. So maybe not. \n\nIf I discard W2, remaining: W1, T1, T3, T6. Still no pairs. \n\nDiscard T3: remaining W1, W2, T1, T6. Still no pairs. \n\nDiscard T1: remaining W1, W2, T3, T6. 